Why the Rate You Lock In Matters So Much
A mortgage isn't just a one-time transaction — it's a financial commitment that often stretches across 15 to 30 years. Even a small difference in your interest rate can add up to tens of thousands of dollars over the life of the loan. Yet many buyers still treat their rate as fixed and non-negotiable, accepting the first number they're quoted without understanding what's actually shaping it.
In reality, mortgage rates are influenced by several factors that buyers have more control over than they realize:
    • Credit score — stronger scores typically unlock lower rates 
    • Loan type — conventional, FHA, VA, and USDA loans each carry different rate structures 
    • Down payment size — a larger down payment can reduce both your rate and monthly payment 
    • Debt-to-income ratio — lenders evaluate how much of your income is already committed elsewhere 
    • Loan term — shorter terms, like 15-year loans, often come with lower rates than 30-year terms 
Families in Decatur who take time to understand these factors before applying often end up in a noticeably stronger position than those who simply accept whatever rate is first presented to them.
